/*!
\page mocking Mocking and stubs

Mocking within programming languages that have access to reflection is a very common practice,
especially when it comes to testing, and unit testing frameworks. Although there are many different
testing frameworks that implement mocking in C(_GoogleTest and CMock_), they all are fairly strict,
or require a lot of compilation time to run tests; parsing specific binary formats like ELF-only, or
recompiles your entire source code just to replace a single function, required that you
build-system is supported, etc.

The entire idea behind this project is to provide a simple and easy to use mocking framework that
can be used with any build-system, on any POSIX-compliant system, any architecture, and in any
version greater than C99. Mocking can be simplified using binary-patching and stubs, in the case of
`tapi`, we use this to our advantage by using runtime-patching to replace call targets with stubs,
isolating test functions.

For example, on a x86-64 system, we can replace the call to `get_some_value` at address 0x7ff12300
with a stub `mock_get_some_value` at address 0x7ff45600 returns a fixed value,

```asm
; get_some_value(int x) {
push %rbp
movq %rsp, %rbp
movq %edi, -0x4(%rbp) ; ~x + 1
movq -0x4(%rbp), %eax
neg %eax
pop %rbp
ret

; target_function(...) {
push %rbp
movq %rsp, %rbp
; ...
call get_some_value ; e8 7f f1 23 00 <- this can be replaced with our stub address.
; ...
pop %rbp
ret ; }
```

Where the relative jump address to `get_some_value` can be replaced with,

```asm
; target_function(...) {
push %rbp
movq %rsp, %rbp
; ...
call mock_get_some_value ; e8 7f f4 56 00 <- the address of our stub!
; ...
pop %rbp
ret ; }
```

In order to make this work, we need to make use of a runtime-disassembler to find the address of
`target_function`, and then patch the first instruction of `target_function` to call our stub. The
runtime-disassembler used within this project is `capstone`, a powerful disassembler for any
modern architecture (_to learn more about capstone, visit
[https://www.capstone-engine.org/](https://www.capstone-engine.org/)_). This same process can be
used to replace any function call, across any binary, across any architecture and operating system.
*/